The module uses the paper’s native bipartite matchings and exact \(\lfloor5p/3\rfloor\) regime from [Theorem 2.1](https://arxiv.org/html/1305.1466v2). It declares Track B: Hopcroft–Karp solves the fixed-matching incidence problem in 2,443 measured edge scans, while the planted affine route uses 266 exact operations.

Local results:

- All gates G1–G9(c) pass.
- Random guesses: 0/200,000.
- Four attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference algorithm: 8/8, as expected.
- Answer: 319 characters, 107 atoms.
- Canonicalization: 80/80 invariance checks; 20/20 unrelated instances distinct.

All three required oracle runs were attempted, but OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” before inference. Those errors are preserved honestly and are not counted as model failures. No `REJECTED.md` was created because this is an infrastructure block, not a failure of G, H, or V.
