   129	NOTES = r"""
   130	Definition and theorem regime. Section 1 defines a (partial) rainbow matching
   131	as a choice of pairwise-disjoint edges from distinct members F_i of the family;
   132	it is full when every F_i is represented. Theorem 2.1 states that p matchings,
   133	each of size floor(5p/3), in a bipartite graph have a full rainbow matching.
   134	This generator hands the solver exactly those objects. It additionally points
   135	out a particular p-edge matching R and asks for the bijection proving that R
   136	is full rainbow. Each F_c has exactly floor(5p/3) edges and is itself a matching.
   137	
   138	Step-0 hardness decision. This is not Track A. For a fixed R, certification is
   139	ordinary perfect matching in the incidence graph whose left side is E(R), whose
   140	right side is the family F, and whose adjacencies are edge memberships. The
   141	standard Hopcroft--Karp algorithm is O(E sqrt(V)) and the self-test runs it.
   142	Theorem 2.1's proof is also organized around a maximum partial rainbow matching
   143	and explicit alternating replacements (Proposition 2.1 and Claims 1--3), rather
   144	than supplying an average-case hardness claim. Track B is the honest label.
   145	
   146	Inverse generation. For prime p, sample nonzero a and b modulo p before drawing
   147	any decoys. Put B_(a*c+b) into F_c; then add same-marginal uniform distinct B
   148	edges and private padding edges. The answer assigns colour a^{-1}(t-b) to B_t.
   149	The first six incidence rows are rejection-sampled only to eliminate accidental
   150	second affine explanations of an already known certificate. No answer is found
   151	by solving the finished instance.
   152	
   153	Easy regimes and attacks. Section 1 notes the direct greedy bound g(p)<=2p-1;
   154	the paper improves existence, not computational hardness. The panel therefore
   155	tests incidence-frequency outliers, deterministic first fit, 256 random
   156	fixed-order greedy restarts, and the obvious slopes +/-1 and +/-2. Non-demo
   157	plants exclude those four slopes and all displayed decoys have the same uniform
   158	per-row marginal as the plant. Hopcroft--Karp is reported separately and is
   159	expected to solve every instance on Track B.
   160	
   161	Canonicalization. Arbitrary colour relabelling, arbitrary relabelling of the
   162	edges of R, row order, and within-row order preserve the problem. Exact graph
   163	isomorphism for the resulting incidence graph is not known to be cheap, so the
   164	key uses an eight-round bipartite Weisfeiler--Lehman invariant plus degree and
   165	edge-colour histograms. The self-test proves invariance under the named maps and
   166	checks distinctness; the key can collide on non-isomorphic incidence graphs.
   167	""".strip()
